159	\usepackage{makecell}
160	\usepackage{hyperref}
161	\newcommand{\vna}{LibreVNA}
162	
163	\title{LibreVNA USB Protocol\\\small{Version 12}}
164	
165	\begin{document}
166	\maketitle
167	\tableofcontents
168	\clearpage
169	
170	\section{USB device}
171	The LibreVNA implements a ``custom class'' USB device. It uses a VID of 0x0483 and a PID of 0x4121. The custom class contains a single interface with three bulk endpoints:
172	\begin{itemize}
173	\item \textbf{Endpoint 0x01:} Communication data from the USB host to the LibreVNA
174	\item \textbf{Endpoint 0x81:} Communication data from the LibreVNA to the USB host
175	\item \textbf{Endpoint 0x82:} Debug messages from the LibreVNA
176	\end{itemize}
177	
178	Endpoint 0x82 is exclusively used for debug messages. They are transmitted in ASCII format. All protocol packets described in this document are always transmitted over endpoints 0x01 and 0x81.
179	
180	\section{General packet structure}
181	The USB traffic through bulk endpoints can be viewed as a stream of bytes. The communication between the LibreVNA and the USB host is done in packets. To detect the packets within the data stream, some framing is needed. This general package structure is described in this section.
182	
183	Each packet consists of the following fields:
184	\begin{enumerate}
185	\item \textbf{Header:} 1 byte, always 0x5A
186	\item \textbf{Length:} 2 bytes, length of the overall packet in bytes, including the header and the checksum
187	\item \textbf{Type:} 1 byte, defines the type of packet and subsequently the data encoding within the payload
188	\item \textbf{Payload:} Any amount of bytes, content depends on the packet type
189	\item \textbf{CRC:} 4 bytes, CRC32 over all other packet bytes (header, length, type and payload)
190	\end{enumerate}
191	\noindent
192	All values in the USB protocol are little-endian.
193	
194	\section{Packet types}
195	The following packet types are available:
196	\begin{ThreePartTable}
197	\setlength\tabcolsep{3pt}
198	
199	\begin{TableNotes}
200	  \item[a] Direction of packet transfer:
201	  \begin{footnotesize}
202	  \begin{itemize}
203	  \item \textbf{D$\rightarrow$H:} Device to host
204	  \item \textbf{H$\rightarrow$D:} Host to device
205	  \item \textbf{D$\leftrightarrow$H:} Both directions used
206	  \end{itemize}
207	  \end{footnotesize}
208	  \item[b] Packet type that will be sent in response to this packet
209	  \item[c] The response will be sent multiple times
210	\end{TableNotes}
211	
212	\begin{longtable}{p{0.06\textwidth} |  p{0.3\textwidth} | p{0.07\textwidth} | p{0.4\textwidth} |p{0.1\textwidth} }
213	\toprule
214	\textbf{Type} &\textbf{Name}  & \textbf{Dir}\tnote{a} &\textbf{Description} &\textbf{Answer}\tnote{a} \\ 
215	\hline
216	\endhead
217	\midrule[\heavyrulewidth]
218	\endfoot  
219	\midrule[\heavyrulewidth]
220	\insertTableNotes  % tell LaTeX where to insert the table-related notes
221	\endlastfoot
222	
223	2 & SweepSettings & H$\rightarrow$D & Sets the sweep paramaters and starts the sweep in VNA mode& 27\tnote{c}\\
224	3 & ManualStatusV1 & D$\rightarrow$H & Contains the hardware status when in manual control mode & None\\
225	4 & ManualControlV1 & H$\rightarrow$D & Transfers the manual control configuration, switches the device into manual control mode & 3\tnote{c}\\
226	5 & DeviceInfo & D$\rightarrow$H & Contains the device information (firmware/hardware version, capabilities,...)&None \\
227	6 & FirmwarePacket & H$\rightarrow$D & Contains a piece of firmware data &None \\
228	7 & Ack & D$\rightarrow$H & Sent as a response to every successfully received and handled packet&None \\
229	8 & ClearFlash & H$\rightarrow$D & Triggers the flash erase procedure. Must be issues before transferring firmware data&None \\
230	9 & PerformFirmwareUpdate & H$\rightarrow$D & Triggers the firmware update once all firmware data has been transferred&None \\
231	10 & Nack & D$\rightarrow$H & Sent as a response to every unknown command or failure to execute the requested command&None \\
232	11 & Reference & H$\rightarrow$D & Configure the external/internal reference &None \\
233	12 & Generator & H$\rightarrow$D & Switches the VNA into generator mode and configures the generator output&None \\
234	13 & SpectrumAnalyzerSettings & H$\rightarrow$D & Sets the sweep parameters and starts the sweep in spectrum analyzer mode&14\tnote{c} \\
235	14 & SpectrumAnalyzerResult & D$\rightarrow$H & Sent for every sampled frequency within the sweep in spectrum analyzer mode&None \\
236	15 & RequestDeviceInfo & H$\rightarrow$D & Makes the device send the DeviceInfo packet &5 \\
237	16 & RequestSourceCal & H$\rightarrow$D & Makes the device send the source calibration packets & 18\tnote{c}\\
238	17 & RequestReceiverCal & H$\rightarrow$D & Makes the device send the receiver calibration packets & 19\tnote{c}\\
239	18 & SourceCalPoint & D$\leftrightarrow$H & Contains a single source amplitude calibration point &None\\
240	19 & ReceiverCalPoint & D$\leftrightarrow$H & Contains a single receiver amplitude calibration point &None \\
241	20 & SetIdle & H$\rightarrow$D & Stops all device activity & None \\
242	21 & RequestFrequencyCorrection & H$\rightarrow$D & Makes the device send the frequency calibration packet &22 \\
243	22 & FrequencyCorrection & D$\leftrightarrow$H & Contains the frequency calibration factor & None\\
244	23 & RequestAcquisition-FrequencySettings & H$\rightarrow$D & Makes the device send the acquisition frequency settings & 24\\
245	24 & AcquisitionFrequencySettings & D$\rightarrow$H & Contains the configuration of IF and sample frequencies &None \\
246	25 & DeviceStatusV1 & D$\rightarrow$H & Contains the hardware device status (lock, temperatures,...) &None \\
247	26 & RequestDeviceStatus & H$\rightarrow$D & Makes the device send the device status &25 \\
248	27 & VNADatapoint & D$\rightarrow$H & Sent for every sampled frequency within the sweep in VNA mode &None \\
249	28 & SetTrigger & D$\leftrightarrow$H & Updates the trigger status for synchronization over USB & None\\
250	29 & ClearTrigger & D$\leftrightarrow$H & Updates the trigger status for synchronization over USB & None\\
251	30 & StopStatusUpdates & H$\rightarrow$D & Stops the automatic transmission of device status packets & None\\
252	31 & StartStatusUpdates & H$\rightarrow$D & Starts the automatic transmission of device status packets & None\\
253	32 & InitiateSweep & H$\rightarrow$D & Initiates a single sweep when configured for standby operation & None\\
254	\end{longtable}   
255	\end{ThreePartTable}
256	An Ack is transmitted by the device for every received command after it has been handled successfully. If additional responses are triggered by the command, they are transmitted after this Ack.
257	
258	Received packets from the device are not acknowledged by the host; the host never sends an Ack packet.
259	
260	\subsection{SweepSettings}
261	Transmitting this packet will switch the LibreVNA into VNA mode and start the sweep. During the sweep, VNADatapoint packets are generated for each completed point in the sweep.
262	
263	The sampling for each frequency (or power) point in the sweep is done in stages. In each stage, the stimulus can be active at another port. A typical full two-port sweep would therefore use two stages, with the stimulus being active on port 1 during stage 0 and on port 2 during stage 1. For faster measurements, this could be reduced to a single stage if only a subset of the S-parameters is required. Similarly, more than two stages can be used (with the stimulus inactive during some) when multiple devices are synchronized. Another device in the setup will have to generate the stimulus during the inactive stages.
264	
265	
266	The packet contains the following fields:
267	\begin{ThreePartTable}
268	\setlength\tabcolsep{3pt}
269	
270	\begin{longtable}{p{0.08\textwidth} |  p{0.08\textwidth}  |  p{0.1\textwidth}| p{0.25\textwidth} | p{0.43\textwidth}}
271	\toprule
272	\textbf{Offset} &\textbf{Length} &\textbf{Type} & \textbf{Name} &\textbf{Description} \\ 
273	\hline
274	\endhead
275	\midrule[\heavyrulewidth]
276	\endfoot  
277	\midrule[\heavyrulewidth]
278	%\insertTableNotes  % tell LaTeX where to insert the table-related notes
279	\endlastfoot
280	
281	0 & 8 & UINT64 & f\_start & Start frequency in Hz \\
282	8 & 8 & UINT64 & f\_stop & Stop frequency in Hz \\
283	16 & 2 & UINT16 & points & Number of points in the sweep \\
284	18 & 4 & UINT32 & IF\_bandwidth & Bandwidth of the IF sampling in Hz \\
285	22 & 2 &  INT16 &cdbm\_excitation\_start &  Stimulus power at the first point in $\frac{1}{100}$dBm \\
286	24 & 2 & UINT16 & Configuration & Bitmap for configuration, see below \\
287	26 & 2 &  INT16 &cdbm\_excitation\_stop & Stimulus power at the last point in $\frac{1}{100}$dBm \\
288	\end{longtable}   
289	\end{ThreePartTable}
290	
291	\paragraph{Configuration:}
292	\begin{center}
293	\begin{tikzpicture}
294	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
295	\rwbits{0}{2}{syncMode}
296	\rwbits{2}{3}{P2 Stage}
297	\rwbits{5}{3}{P1 Stage}
298	\rwbits{8}{3}{Stages}
299	\rwbits{11}{1}{LOG}
300	\rwbits{12}{1}{FP}
301	\rwbits{13}{1}{SP}
302	\rwbits{14}{1}{SM}
303	\rwbits{15}{1}{SO}
304	\end{tikzpicture}
305	\end{center}
306	\begin{itemize}
307	\item \textbf{syncMode:} Synchronization mode when multiple devices are used together
308	\begin{center}
309	\begin{tabular}{ c|c }
310	Setting & Synchronization\\
311	 \hline
312	00 & Disabled \\
313	01 & USB\\
314	10 & External reference\\
315	11 & External trigger\\
316	\end{tabular}
317	\end{center}
318	\item \textbf{P1 Stage:} Sets the stage number when the stimulus is active at port 1. Stage number indizes start at 0.
319	\item \textbf{P2 Stage:} Sets the stage number when the stimulus is active at port 2. Stage number indizes start at 0.
320	\item \textbf{Stages:} Sets the number of used stages. The number of stages is one more than this value. E.g. set to 1 for 2 stages
321	\item \textbf{LOG:} Set for a logarithmic sweep (only for frequency, power adjustment during the sweep is always linear)
322	\item \textbf{FP:} Fixed power setting. This must be enabled for power sweeps (when  cdbm\_excitation\_start $\neq$  cdbm\_excitation\_stop)
323	\begin{center}
324	\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{ c|X }
325	Setting & Behavior\\
326	 \hline
327	0 & Attenuator setting is fixed during the sweep. This will result in inaccurate stimulus level but prevent discrete jumps in output power. \\
328	1 & Attenuator setting is changed during the sweep. This will result in more accurate stimulus level but also create discrete jumps in output power. \\
329	\end{tabularx}
330	\end{center}
331	\item \textbf{SP:} Suppress peaks. Recommended setting: always enabled.
332	\begin{center}
333	\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{ c|X }
334	Setting & Behavior\\
335	 \hline
336	0 & 2.LO is adjusted to compensate for limited frequency resolution in 1.LO. Slight decrease in maximum sweep speed. \\
337	1 & 2.LO is kept at its nominal value. Slightly faster sweep but this will result in peaks at frequencies where the 1.LO it too far off the ideal frequency. \\
338	\end{tabularx}
339	\end{center}
340	\item \textbf{SM:} Sync Master. Must be set to 1 at exactly one device when multiple devices are synchronized. Set to 0 when synchronization is disabled.
341	\item \textbf{SO:} Standby Operation. Indicates whether the VNA will begin sweep immediately, or wait in the configured state to be triggered manually by InitiateSweep packets. Standy operation allows for lower latency of intermittent single sweeps.
342	\begin{center}
343	\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{ c|X }
344	Setting & Behavior\\
345	 \hline
346	0 & VNA will begin sweep immediately and timeout to idle mode 1000ms after sweep is completed or 100ms after entering the halted state. \\
347	1 & VNA will wait in a configured state for InitiateSweep packets. The host application is responsible for putting the VNA into idle mode with a SetIdle packet.\\
348	\end{tabularx}
349	\end{center}
350	\end{itemize}
351	
352	\subsection{ManualStatusV1}
353	This packet is generated by the LibreVNA 1.0 when in manual control mode. It is transmitted in regular intervals on its own.
354	
355	The packet contains the following fields:
356	\begin{ThreePartTable}
357	\setlength\tabcolsep{3pt}
358	
359	\begin{longtable}{p{0.08\textwidth} |  p{0.08\textwidth}  |  p{0.1\textwidth}| p{0.15\textwidth} | p{0.53\textwidth}}
360	\toprule
361	\textbf{Offset} &\textbf{Length} &\textbf{Type} & \textbf{Name} &\textbf{Description} \\ 
362	\hline
363	\endhead
364	\midrule[\heavyrulewidth]
365	\endfoot  
366	\midrule[\heavyrulewidth]
367	%\insertTableNotes  % tell LaTeX where to insert the table-related notes
368	\endlastfoot
369	
370	0 & 2 & INT16 & port1min & Minimum value of the ADC at port 1 \\
371	2 & 2 & INT16 & port1max & Maximum value of the ADC at port 1 \\
372	4 & 2 & INT16 & port2min & Minimum value of the ADC at port 2 \\
373	6 & 2 & INT16 & port2max & Maximum value of the ADC at port 2 \\
374	8 & 2 & INT16 & refmin & Minimum value of the ADC at the reference receiver \\
375	10 & 2 & INT16 & refmax & Maximum value of the ADC at the reference receiver \\
376	12 & 4 & FLOAT & port1real & Real part of the complex signal at port 1 \\
377	16 & 4 & FLOAT & port1imag & Imaginary part of the complex signal at port 1 \\
378	20 & 4 & FLOAT & port2real & Real part of the complex signal at port 2 \\
379	24 & 4 & FLOAT & port2imag & Imaginary part of the complex signal at port 2 \\
380	28 & 4 & FLOAT & refreal & Real part of the complex signal at the reference receiver \\
381	32 & 4 & FLOAT & refimag & Imaginary part of the complex signal at the reference receiver \\
382	36 & 1 & UINT8 & temp\_source & Temperature of the source PLL in \si{\celsius} \\
383	37 & 1 & UINT8 & temp\_LO & Temperature of the LO PLL in \si{\celsius} \\
384	38 & 1 & UINT8 & Lock status & Bit 0: lock status of source PLL. Bit 1: lock status of LO PLL \\
385	\end{longtable}   
