927	\item \textbf{ULV:} Unlevel. The requested output signal amplitude can not be reached. This is not actually measured and based on calculations only.
928	\item \textbf{OVL:} ADC overload. The amplitude of at least one of the ADCs reached the non-linear region and the signal level can not be trusted.
929	\item \textbf{LLO:} 1.LO locked.
930	\item \textbf{SLO:} Source locked.
931	\item \textbf{FC:} FPGA successfully configured.
932	\item \textbf{ERU:} External reference used. The external reference input is used for all PLLs.
933	\item \textbf{ERA:} External reference available. A signal is detected at the external reference input.
934	\end{itemize}
935	
936	\subsection{RequestDeviceStatus}
937	This packet is used to make the device send the DeviceStatusV1 packet. It has no payload.
938	
939	\subsection{VNADatapoint}
940	The VNADatapoint packet is generated by the device for every completed sweep point when in VNA mode.
941	\begin{important}
942	This packet has the CRC set to 0x00000000 as the CRC calculation would take too long when using high IF bandwidths.
943	\end{important}
944	
945	The packet contains the following fields:
946	\begin{ThreePartTable}
947	\setlength\tabcolsep{3pt}
948	
949	\begin{longtable}{p{0.08\textwidth} |  p{0.08\textwidth}  |  p{0.1\textwidth}| p{0.25\textwidth} | p{0.43\textwidth}}
950	\toprule
951	\textbf{Offset} &\textbf{Length} &\textbf{Type} & \textbf{Name} &\textbf{Description} \\ 
952	\hline
953	\endhead
954	\midrule[\heavyrulewidth]
955	\endfoot  
956	\midrule[\heavyrulewidth]
957	%\insertTableNotes  % tell LaTeX where to insert the table-related notes
958	\endlastfoot
959	
960	0 & 8 & UINT64 & Frequency & Frequency of the sweep point in Hz\\
961	8 & 2 & INT16 & PowerLevel & Stimulus level of the sweep point in $\frac{1}{100}$dBm \\
962	10 & 2 & UINT16 & PointNumber & Number of this point in the sweep \\
963	12 & 4*x & Array of FLOAT & Real values & The real parts of a variable amount of receiver data \\
964	12+4*x & 4*x & Array of FLOAT & Imag values & The imaginary parts of a variable amount of receiver data \\
965	12+8*x & 1*x & UINT8 & Array of UINT8 & Variable data description \\
966	\end{longtable}   
967	\end{ThreePartTable}
968	
969	The sampling data consists of a variable amount of values. The amount of values depend on the amount of configured stages and also on the hardware architecture (might change in the future). The VNADatapoint contains three arrays of equal length. Two of the arrays contain the real and imaginary parts of the acquired data. The third array contains a bitmask for every value, describing the content. The length of all arrays is not explicitly transmitted and must be inferred from the overall packet length.
970	
971	
972	\paragraph{Data description bitmask:}
973	\begin{center}
974	\begin{tikzpicture}
975	\bitrect{8}{8-\bit}
976	\rwbits{0}{3}{Stage}
977	\rwbits{3}{1}{Ref}
978	\rwbits{4}{1}{P4}
979	\rwbits{5}{1}{P3}
980	\rwbits{6}{1}{P2}
981	\rwbits{7}{1}{P1}
982	\end{tikzpicture}
983	\end{center}
984	\begin{itemize}
985	\item \textbf{Stage:} The active stage when the value was acquired. The port on which the stimulus was active during this stage is known from the SweepSettings packet that was used to set up the currently active sweep.
986	\item \textbf{Ref:} The value is from a reference receiver.
987	\item \textbf{P4:} The value is from a port 4 receiver.
988	\item \textbf{P3:} The value is from a port 3 receiver.
989	\item \textbf{P2:} The value is from a port 2 receiver.
990	\item \textbf{P1:} The value is from a port 1 receiver.
991	\end{itemize}
992	As the LibreVNA 1.0 only has two ports, P3 and P4 are never used and reserved for future developments.
993	
994	In case of a three receiver architecture (as the LibreVNA 1.0 has), multiple port bits can be set for reference receiver values. For a typical full two-port sweep, the LibreVNA 1.0 will generate six values for every sweep point:
995	\begin{ThreePartTable}
996	\setlength\tabcolsep{3pt}
997	
998	\begin{longtable}{p{0.08\textwidth} |  p{0.1\textwidth}  |  p{0.6\textwidth}}
999	\toprule
1000	\textbf{\#} &\textbf{Bitmask} &\textbf{Content} \\ 
1001	\hline
1002	\endhead
1003	\midrule[\heavyrulewidth]
1004	\endfoot  
1005	\midrule[\heavyrulewidth]
1006	%\insertTableNotes  % tell LaTeX where to insert the table-related notes
1007	\endlastfoot
1008	
1009	1 & 0x01 & Port 1 receiver signal during stage 0 \\
1010	2 & 0x02 & Port 2 receiver signal during stage 0 \\
1011	3 & 0x13 & Reference receiver signal during stage 0 \\
1012	4 & 0x21 & Port 1 receiver signal during stage 1 \\
1013	5 & 0x22 & Port 2 receiver signal during stage 1 \\
1014	6 & 0x33 & Reference receiver signal during stage 1 \\
1015	\end{longtable}   
1016	\end{ThreePartTable}
1017	
1018	
1019	\subsection{SetTrigger}
1020	This packet is used when multiple devices are synchronized over USB and can be transmitted in both directions. It has no payload. Synchronized devices must be logically organized in a closed loop. When a SetTrigger packet is received from any devices in the loop it must be passed on to the next device in the loop.
1021	
1022	\subsection{ClearTrigger}
1023	This packet is used when multiple devices are synchronized over USB and can be transmitted in both directions. It has no payload. Synchronized devices must be logically organized in a closed loop. When a ClearTrigger packet is received from any devices in the loop it must be passed on to the next device in the loop.
1024	
1025	\subsection{StopStatusUpdates}
1026	This packet instructs the device to stop sending automatically scheduled DeviceStatusV1 packets. Device status can still be requested explicitly via RequestDeviceStatus packets.
1027	
1028	\subsection{StartStatusUpdates}
1029	This packet instructs the device to start sending automatically scheduled DeviceStatusV1 packets. This restores default update behaviour if a StopStatusUpdates packet has previously been sent.
1030	
1031	\subsection{InitiateSweep}
1032	This packet instructs the device to initiate a new single sweep when the VNA is configured for standby operation. This triggering method can be used for fast intermittent single sweeps with minimum latency. If the SweepSettings are not configured for standby operation, this packet will result in a Nack response.
1033	
1034	\end{document}
1035

