170	\section{Introduction}
171	This document describes the device protocol of the LibreVNA. This is the protocol used by the LibreVNA to communicate with the LibreVNA-GUI (or other custom implementations). In the context of this document, the LibreVNA is also referred to as the ``device'' and the LibreVNA-GUI as the ``host''.
172	
173	\section{Hardware interface}
174	Depending on the LibreVNA, different hardware interfaces may be used for the implementation of this protocol.
175	
176	\subsection{USB device}
177	The LibreVNA implements a ``custom class'' USB device. It uses a VID of 0x1209 and a PID of 0x4121. The custom class contains a single interface with three bulk endpoints:
178	\begin{itemize}
179	\item \textbf{Endpoint 0x01:} Communication data from the USB host to the LibreVNA
180	\item \textbf{Endpoint 0x81:} Communication data from the LibreVNA to the USB host
181	\item \textbf{Endpoint 0x82:} Debug messages from the LibreVNA
182	\end{itemize}
183	
184	Endpoint 0x82 is exclusively used for debug messages. They are transmitted in ASCII format. All protocol packets described in this document are always transmitted over endpoints 0x01 and 0x81.
185	
186	\subsection{Ethernet interface}
187	The ethernet interface implements two TCP servers, one for protocol data and one for debug messages:
188	\begin{itemize}
189	\item \textbf{Port 19544:} Data interface
190	\item \textbf{Port 19545:} Debug interface
191	\end{itemize}
192	Each server only supports a single connection. If another connection request is received, the existing connection is closed before accepting the new one.
193	
194	Incoming data on the debug interface is ignored. Debug messages are transmitted in ASCII format. All protocol packets described in this document are always transmitted over the data interface.
195	
196	\subsubsection{Device Discovery}
197	Initially, the IP addresses of connected LibreVNAs may not be known. To automatically detect any devices, the LibreVNA implements SSDP and responds to M-SEARCH packets looking for either
198	\begin{lstlisting}
199	ssdp:all
200	\end{lstlisting}
201	or
202	\begin{lstlisting}
203	urn:schemas-upnp-org:device:LibreVNA:1
204	\end{lstlisting}
205	services.
206	
207	
208	\section{General packet structure}
209	The data traffic can be viewed as a stream of bytes. The communication between the LibreVNA and the host is done in packets. To detect the packets within the data stream, some framing is needed. This general package structure is described in this section.
210	
211	Each packet consists of the following fields:
212	\begin{enumerate}
213	\item \textbf{Header:} 1 byte, always 0x5A
214	\item \textbf{Length:} 2 bytes, length of the overall packet in bytes, including the header and the checksum
215	\item \textbf{Type:} 1 byte, defines the type of packet and subsequently the data encoding within the payload
216	\item \textbf{Payload:} Any amount of bytes, content depends on the packet type
217	\item \textbf{CRC:} 4 bytes, CRC32 over all other packet bytes (header, length, type and payload)
218	\end{enumerate}
219	\noindent
220	All values in the device protocol are little-endian.
221	
