1266	\subsection{RequestDeviceStatus}
1267	This packet is used to make the device send the DeviceStatus packet. It has no payload.
1268	
1269	\subsection{VNADatapoint}
1270	The VNADatapoint packet is generated by the device for every completed sweep point when in VNA mode.
1271	\begin{important}
1272	This packet has the CRC set to 0x00000000 as the CRC calculation would take too long when using high IF bandwidths.
1273	\end{important}
1274	
1275	The packet contains the following fields:
1276	\begin{ThreePartTable}
1277	\setlength\tabcolsep{3pt}
1278	
1279	\begin{longtable}{p{0.08\textwidth} |  p{0.08\textwidth}  |  p{0.1\textwidth}| p{0.25\textwidth} | p{0.43\textwidth}}
1280	\toprule
1281	\textbf{Offset} &\textbf{Length} &\textbf{Type} & \textbf{Name} &\textbf{Description} \\ 
1282	\hline
1283	\endhead
1284	\midrule[\heavyrulewidth]
1285	\endfoot  
1286	\midrule[\heavyrulewidth]
1287	%\insertTableNotes  % tell LaTeX where to insert the table-related notes
1288	\endlastfoot
1289	
1290	0 & 8 & UINT64 & Frequency & Frequency of the sweep point in Hz\\
1291	8 & 2 & INT16 & PowerLevel & Stimulus level of the sweep point in $\frac{1}{100}$dBm \\
1292	10 & 2 & UINT16 & PointNumber & Number of this point in the sweep \\
1293	12 & 4*x & Array of FLOAT & Real values & The real parts of a variable amount of receiver data \\
1294	12+4*x & 4*x & Array of FLOAT & Imag values & The imaginary parts of a variable amount of receiver data \\
1295	12+8*x & 1*x & UINT8 & Array of UINT8 & Variable amount of data description bitmasks\\
1296	\end{longtable}   
1297	\end{ThreePartTable}
1298	
1299	The sampling data consists of a variable amount of values. The amount of values depend on the amount of configured stages and also on the hardware architecture (might change in the future). The VNADatapoint contains three arrays of equal length. Two of the arrays contain the real and imaginary parts of the acquired data. The third array contains a bitmask for every value, describing the content. The length of all arrays is not explicitly transmitted and must be inferred from the overall packet length.
1300	
1301	
1302	\paragraph{Data description bitmask:}
1303	\begin{center}
1304	\begin{tikzpicture}
1305	\bitrect{8}{8-\bit}
1306	\rwbits{0}{3}{Stage}
1307	\rwbits{3}{1}{Ref}
1308	\rwbits{4}{1}{P4}
1309	\rwbits{5}{1}{P3}
1310	\rwbits{6}{1}{P2}
1311	\rwbits{7}{1}{P1}
1312	\end{tikzpicture}
1313	\end{center}
1314	\begin{itemize}
1315	\item \textbf{Stage:} The active stage when the value was acquired. The port on which the stimulus was active during this stage is known from the SweepSettings packet that was used to set up the currently active sweep.
1316	\item \textbf{Ref:} The value is from a reference receiver.
1317	\item \textbf{P4:} The value is from a port 4 receiver.
1318	\item \textbf{P3:} The value is from a port 3 receiver.
1319	\item \textbf{P2:} The value is from a port 2 receiver.
1320	\item \textbf{P1:} The value is from a port 1 receiver.
1321	\end{itemize}
1322	In case of a three receiver architecture (as the LibreVNA 1.0 has), multiple port bits can be set for reference receiver values. For a typical full two-port sweep, the LibreVNA 1.0 will generate six values for every sweep point:
1323	\begin{ThreePartTable}
1324	\setlength\tabcolsep{3pt}
1325	
1326	\begin{longtable}{p{0.08\textwidth} |  p{0.1\textwidth}  |  p{0.6\textwidth}}
1327	\toprule
1328	\textbf{\#} &\textbf{Bitmask} &\textbf{Content} \\ 
1329	\hline
1330	\endhead
1331	\midrule[\heavyrulewidth]
1332	\endfoot  
1333	\midrule[\heavyrulewidth]
1334	%\insertTableNotes  % tell LaTeX where to insert the table-related notes
1335	\endlastfoot
1336	
1337	1 & 0x01 & Port 1 receiver signal during stage 0 \\
1338	2 & 0x02 & Port 2 receiver signal during stage 0 \\
1339	3 & 0x13 & Reference receiver signal during stage 0 \\
1340	4 & 0x21 & Port 1 receiver signal during stage 1 \\
1341	5 & 0x22 & Port 2 receiver signal during stage 1 \\
1342	6 & 0x33 & Reference receiver signal during stage 1 \\
1343	\end{longtable}   
1344	\end{ThreePartTable}
1345	
1346	The host must assemble the S-parameter data from these receiver values. This calculation has to be offloaded to the host because the reference and port receiver measurements may be split across different devices when synchronizing multiple LibreVNAs.
1347	
1348	\paragraph{Example procedure to assemble S21}
1349	Some definitions and assumptions:
1350	\begin{itemize}
1351	\item S21 is the through measurement from port 1 to port 2, meaning we need the reference receiver data from port 1 and the port receiver data from port 2
1352	\item Port 1 had the stimulus signal at stage 0 and port 2 had the stimulus signal at stage 1. This is the default for a full two-port sweep. If configured differently in the SweepSettings packet, adjust the stage values accordingly
1353	\end{itemize}
1354	
1355	\hfill\newline
1356	The host must perform the following operations:
1357	\begin{enumerate}
1358	\item Wait for reception of a VNADatapoint packet
1359	\item Determine the array length of the received data: $$array\_length = (packet\_size - 12) / 9$$ 
1360	\item Find the port receiver data of port 2 for the correct stage (when port 1 had the stimulus, in this example stage 0)
1361	\begin{enumerate}
1362	\item Iterate over all data description bitmasks in the VNADatapoint
1363	\item Find the one with bitmask 0b0000xx1x (stage 0, port 2, not a reference receiver measurement). Bits marked ``x'' are ``don't care''.
1364	\item Note the index $n$ of this data description bitmask in the data description bitmask array
1365	\item Use the index $n$ to get the real and imaginary values of the port receiver data from the real and imaginary arrays: $$port\_receiver = Real\_values[n] + i * Imag\_values[n]$$
1366	\end{enumerate}
1367	\item Find the reference receiver data of port 1 for the correct stage (when port 1 had the stimulus, in this example stage 0)
1368	\begin{enumerate}
1369	\item Iterate over all data description bitmasks in the VNADatapoint
1370	\item Find the one with bitmask 0b0001xxx1 (stage 0, port 1, reference receiver measurement). Bits marked ``x'' are ``don't care''.
1371	\item Note the index $n$ of this data description bitmask in the data description bitmask array
1372	\item Use the index $n$ to get the real and imaginary values of the reference receiver data from the real and imaginary arrays: $$reference\_receiver = Real\_values[n] + i * Imag\_values[n]$$
1373	\end{enumerate}
1374	\item Calculate S21 as the ratio between the port and reference receiver data: $$ S21 = \frac{port\_receiver}{reference\_receiver}$$
1375	\end{enumerate}
1376	
1377	\subsection{SetTrigger}
1378	This packet is used when multiple devices are synchronized over the data protocol and can be transmitted in both directions. It has no payload. Synchronized devices must be logically organized in a closed loop. When a SetTrigger packet is received from any devices in the loop it must be passed on to the next device in the loop.
1379	
1380	\subsection{ClearTrigger}
1381	This packet is used when multiple devices are synchronized over the data protocol and can be transmitted in both directions. It has no payload. Synchronized devices must be logically organized in a closed loop. When a ClearTrigger packet is received from any devices in the loop it must be passed on to the next device in the loop.
1382	
1383	\subsection{StopStatusUpdates}
1384	This packet instructs the device to stop sending automatically scheduled DeviceStatus packets. Device status can still be requested explicitly via RequestDeviceStatus packets.
1385	
1386	\subsection{StartStatusUpdates}
1387	This packet instructs the device to start sending automatically scheduled DeviceStatus packets. This restores default update behaviour if a StopStatusUpdates packet has previously been sent.
1388	
1389	\subsection{InitiateSweep}
1390	This packet instructs the device to initiate a new single sweep when the VNA is configured for standby operation. This triggering method can be used for fast intermittent single sweeps with minimum latency. If the SweepSettings are not configured for standby operation, this packet will result in a Nack response.
1391	
1392	\end{document}
